Thinking: The user wants contour lines on MapLibre GL JS map, generated at runtime from a raster-dem elevation source, not pre-rendered vector contour tiles. They ask if possible and how to approach. Need to answer using the skill maplibre-terrain-patterns. Provide explanation, mention maplibre-contour library, steps, code snippets, considerations, performance, encoding, source config, etc. Also mention alternative approaches, pitfalls, encoding, self-hosting, etc. Provide a clear guide. Should be thorough.

## Yes – you can draw contour lines **directly in the browser** from a `raster‑dem` source.  
The trick is to let a small helper library read the DEM tiles, decode the elevation values, and emit a **virtual vector source** that MapLibre can style like any other line layer.  

The most common, battle‑tested way to do this today is the **`maplibre‑contour`** package (formerly `mapbox‑contour`). It:

| Feature | How it works |
|---------|--------------|
| **Input** | Any `raster‑dem` source (Terrain‑RGB or Terrarium) that you can reach via HTTP/HTTPS or `pmtiles://` |
| **Output** | A MapLibre `vector` source that contains a single layer called `contours` (or any name you give it) with GeoJSON‑like features: `{type:"Feature",properties:{ele:number, level:number},geometry:{type:"LineString",coordinates:[…]}}` |
| **Runtime** | All work happens in a Web‑Worker (or the main thread if you disable it). No pre‑generated contour tiles are required. |
| **Styling** | Use normal `line-*` paint properties, data‑driven styling on `level` or `ele`, etc. |
| **Performance** | Fast enough for most web maps (contours are generated only for the tiles that are actually on‑screen). You can throttle the zoom levels where they appear, or limit the contour interval per zoom. |

Below is a **step‑by‑step guide** that shows you how to set it up, from choosing a DEM source to styling the contour lines. I’ll also cover a few “gotchas” (encoding, tile size, caching, mobile performance) and optional enhancements (multizoom thresholds, custom worker, offline/PMTiles).

---

## 1. Pick a DEM source that supports the encoding you’ll use

| Source | URL pattern | Encoding | Why it’s a good fit for runtime contours |
|--------|-------------|----------|------------------------------------------|
| **AWS Terrain Tiles** (open data) | `https://s3.amazonaws.com/elevation-tiles-prod/terrarium/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | `terrarium` | Free, global, Terrarium‑encoded PNGs (small, 256 px). |
| **Mapterhorn** (PMTiles) | `pmtiles://https://.../terrain.pmtiles` | `terrarium` | Single‑file archive, fast CDN, works offline with the PMTiles protocol. |
| **MapTiler Terrain‑RGB** | `https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY` | `mapbox` | Commercial, high‑resolution, Mapbox‑RGB encoding. |
| **Self‑hosted GeoTIFF → PNG** | `https://myserver.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | whichever you encode | You control the zoom range and tile size. |

> **Key point:** The `encoding` you pass to `maplibre‑contour` **must match** the source’s encoding. If you get it wrong the contour elevations will be wildly off (often looking flat or inverted) and there will be no console error.

### Example DEM source definition (Terrarium)

```json
{
  "type": "raster-dem",
  "url": "https://s3.amazonaws.com/elevation-tiles-prod/terrarium/{z}/{x}/{y}.png",
  "tileSize": 256,
  "encoding": "terrarium"
}
```

Add this source to your style (or create it programmatically – see step 2).

---

## 2. Install & import `maplibre-contour`

If you’re using a bundler (Webpack, Vite, etc.):

```bash
npm install maplibre-contour
```

Then in your JavaScript:

```js
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';
```

If you prefer a plain‑HTML script tag, you can pull the UMD build from a CDN:

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
<script>
  const mlcontour = window.maplibreContour; // global variable
</script>
```

---

## 3. Create a `DemSource` wrapper

`DemSource` is a thin helper that knows how to fetch DEM tiles, decode them, and expose a **contour source** to MapLibre.

```js
// 1️⃣  Create the DEM wrapper
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // URL template – can be any of the patterns above
  url: 'https://s3.amazonaws.com/elevation-tiles-prod/terrarium/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // Must match the source’s encoding
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // Highest zoom you want contours for (optional, defaults to 14)
  maxzoom: 14,
  // Whether to run the heavy work in a Web Worker (recommended)
  worker: true,
  // Optional: custom worker script URL if you need to bundle it yourself
  // workerUrl: '/js/contour-worker.js'
});
```

### What the options mean

| Option | Description | Typical value |
|--------|-------------|---------------|
| `url` | Tile URL template (or `pmtiles://` URI) | see above |
| `encoding` | `"terrarium"` or `"mapbox"` | match your source |
| `maxzoom` | Highest zoom level you’ll request contours for. Higher values increase memory & CPU. | 13‑14 for most web maps |
| `worker` | `true` → run in a dedicated Web Worker (default). `false` → run on the main thread (useful for debugging). | `true` |
| `workerUrl` | If you bundle the worker yourself, point to it. | — |
| `cacheSize` | Number of DEM tiles to keep in memory (default 256). | 256 |

---

## 4. Register the wrapper with MapLibre

`setupMaplibre(map)` tells the wrapper to install a custom protocol handler (`contour://`) that MapLibre can use as a source.

```js
// 2️⃣  Hook it into the map instance
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

> **Why this step?**  
> MapLibre only knows about `raster`, `vector`, `geojson`, etc. The wrapper creates a **virtual vector source** (`contour://contour-source`) that internally pulls DEM tiles, runs the contour algorithm, and returns GeoJSON features. The `setupMaplibre` call registers that protocol with the MapLibre runtime.

---

## 5. Add the **contour source** to the style

Now you can add a *real* source to the map, but its data comes from the wrapper:

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // 3️⃣  Add a source that points to the wrapper’s virtual source
  map.addSource('contour-source', demSource.contourSource({
    // ── Contour generation options ────────────────────────
    // Zoom‑specific contour intervals (in meters)
    thresholds: {
      // zoom: [minorInterval, majorInterval]
      10: [200, 1000],
      11: [100, 500],
      12: [50, 200],
      13: [20, 100],
      14: [10, 50]
    },
    // Property names that will appear on each feature
    elevationKey: 'ele',   // numeric elevation (meters)
    levelKey:     'level', // contour interval index (0 = minor, 1 = major)
    // Optional: name of the internal layer (defaults to "contours")
    contourLayer: 'contours'
  }));
```

### What the `thresholds` object does

* For each zoom level you specify, the library will generate **two sets** of contours:
  * **Minor** (e.g., every 20 m) – `level = 0`
  * **Major** (e.g., every 100 m) – `level = 1`

If you omit a zoom level, the library falls back to the nearest defined level. You can also supply a **single number** (e.g., `thresholds: 50`) to use the same interval at all zooms.

---

## 6. Add a **line layer** that draws the contours

Now treat the source like any other vector source:

```js
  // 4️⃣  Add a line layer that visualises the contours
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'contour-lines',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'contour-source',
    // The internal layer name we gave above (default "contours")
    'source-layer': 'contours',
    minzoom: 10,          // hide at very low zooms (no need for detail)
    maxzoom: 14,
    layout: {
      // Optional: make the lines smoother on high‑dpi screens
      'line-cap': 'round',
      'line-join': 'round'
    },
    paint: {
      // Colour based on major/minor level
      'line-color': [
        'match',
        ['get', 'level'],
        0, 'rgba(255,255,255,0.6)',   // minor – light
        1, 'rgba(255,255,255,0.9)',   // major – brighter
        'rgba(255,255,255,0.4)'       // fallback (should never happen)
      ],
      // Thicker for major contours
      'line-width': [
        'match',
        ['get', 'level'],
        0, 0.8,
        1, 1.5,
        0.5
      ],
      // Optional: label the major contours
      // (requires a separate symbol layer – see step 7)
    }
  });
```

### Optional: label the major contours

If you want the elevation value displayed on the major contours, add a **symbol layer** that reads the `ele` property:

```js
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'contour-labels',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'contour-source',
    'source-layer': 'contours',
    filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1], // only major contours
    layout: {
      'symbol-placement': 'line',
      'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
      'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold', 'Arial Unicode MS Bold'],
      'text-size': 10,
      'text-offset': [0, 0.5],
      'text-allow-overlap': false,
      'text-ignore-placement': false
    },
    paint: {
      'text-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.9)',
      'text-halo-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
      'text-halo-width': 1
    }
  });
```

---

## 7. Fine‑tune performance & UX

### a. Limit the zoom range

Contours are most useful from **zoom 10‑14**. Below that the map is too coarse to see individual lines, and the worker would waste CPU generating them. Use `minzoom`/`maxzoom` on the layer (as above) and/or omit low‑zoom entries from `thresholds`.

### b. Control tile size & exaggeration

If you also enable **3D terrain** (`map.setTerrain()`), the same DEM source will be used for both the mesh and the contours. That’s fine, but keep in mind:

* The contour worker will request DEM tiles at the **same zoom** as the map view. If you set `exaggeration` > 1, the visual height changes but the underlying elevation values stay the same – contours remain accurate.
* If you need **higher‑resolution contours** than the DEM tiles provide, you must increase the DEM source’s `maxzoom` (or host a higher‑zoom tileset). Otherwise the contours will “snap” to the DEM’s pixel grid.

### c. Cache DEM tiles

`DemSource` already caches a configurable number of DEM tiles (`cacheSize`). For low‑memory devices you can lower this (e.g., `cacheSize: 64`). For smoother panning on desktop you can raise it.

```js
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // …
  cacheSize: 512   // keep more tiles in memory
});
```

### d. Use a **Web Worker** (default)

Running the contour algorithm in a worker isolates the heavy CPU work from the UI thread, keeping map interactions smooth. If you see the worker failing (e.g., CSP blocks `blob:` URLs), you can:

* Serve a dedicated worker script (`workerUrl`) from the same origin.
* Disable the worker (`worker: false`) for debugging only.

### e. Offline / PMTiles

If you want the whole thing to work offline (or you want a single‑file archive for easier CDN caching), pack the DEM tiles into a **PMTiles** file and reference it with the `pmtiles://` protocol:

```js
// Assuming you have already added the PMTiles protocol handler
// (see maplibre-pmtiles-patterns skill)
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  url: 'pmtiles://my-terrain.pmtiles',
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  maxzoom: 14,
  worker: true
});
```

The worker will fetch DEM tiles from the PMTiles archive just like from HTTP.

---

## 8. Full Minimal Working Example

Below is a **complete HTML page** that you can copy‑paste into a local file and open in a browser. It uses the public AWS Terrarium tiles, the `maplibre-contour` library from a CDN, and shows both hillshade and runtime contours.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – Runtime Contours</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
  <!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <!-- maplibre-contour (UMD) -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base imagery (optional)
        'sat': {
          type: 'raster',
          url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/satellite/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg?key=YOUR_KEY',
          tileSize: 512
        },
        // DEM source (used later by the contour wrapper)
        'terrain': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://s3.amazonaws.com/elevation-tiles-prod/terrarium/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          tileSize: 256,
          encoding: 'terrarium'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'sat', type: 'raster', source: 'sat' }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 60,
    bearing: -30
  });

  // 2️⃣  When the map is ready, set up terrain & contours
  map.on('load', () => {
    // Enable 3D terrain (optional – you can skip this)
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });

    // Add a sky layer for nicer 3D look
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'sky',
      type: 'sky',
      paint: {
        'sky-type': 'atmosphere',
        'sky-atmosphere-sun': [0, 90],
        'sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity': 15
      }
    });

    // ---- Contour setup -------------------------------------------------
    const mlcontour = window.maplibreContour; // global from CDN

    const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
      url: 'https://s3.amazonaws.com/elevation-tiles-prod/terrarium/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      encoding: 'terrarium',
      maxzoom: 14,
      worker: true
    });

    // Register the protocol handler with MapLibre
    demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

    // Add the virtual contour source
    map.addSource('contour-source', demSource.contourSource({
      thresholds: {
        10: [200, 1000],
        11: [100, 500],
        12: [50, 200],
        13: [20, 100],
        14: [10, 50]
      },
      elevationKey: 'ele',
      levelKey: 'level',
      contourLayer: 'contours'
    }));

    // Contour line layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'contour-source',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      minzoom: 10,
      maxzoom: 14,
      paint: {
        'line-color': [
          'match',
          ['get', 'level'],
          0, 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)',   // minor
          1, 'rgba(255,255,255,0.9)',   // major
          'rgba(255,255,255,0.3)'
        ],
        'line-width': [
          'match',
          ['get', 'level'],
          0, 0.8,
          1, 1.5,
          0.5
        ]
      }
    });

    // Optional label layer for major contours
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'contour-source',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
      layout: {
        'symbol-placement': 'line',
        'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
        'text-size': 10,
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold', 'Arial Unicode MS Bold'],
        'text-offset': [0, 0.5],
        'text-allow-overlap': false
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.9)',
        'text-halo-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
        'text-halo-width': 1
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What you’ll see**

* A satellite basemap draped over 3‑D terrain (optional).
* Light gray minor contour lines and bright white major contour lines.
* Elevation labels (e.g., “120 m”) appearing on the major lines.
* All contours are generated on‑the‑fly from the Terrarium DEM tiles.

---

## 9. Common Pitfalls & How to Fix Them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Contours look flat / all at the same elevation** | Wrong `encoding` (e.g., using `"mapbox"` for Terrarium tiles) | Set `encoding: 'terrarium'` (or `'mapbox'` for Mapbox‑RGB) in both the `raster-dem` source and the `DemSource` config. |
| **No contours appear at any zoom** | `thresholds` missing for the current zoom, or `minzoom`/`maxzoom` on the layer hides them | Add a `thresholds` entry for the zoom you’re testing, or lower the layer’s `minzoom`. |
| **Performance stalls when panning quickly** | Worker not used (or blocked) → heavy work on UI thread | Ensure `worker: true` and that the page’s CSP allows `blob:` URLs, or provide a custom `workerUrl`. |
| **Contours are jagged / look like a pixel grid** | DEM tiles are low‑resolution (e.g., using zoom 5 tiles for zoom 12) | Increase the DEM source’s `maxzoom` (or use a higher‑resolution tileset). |
| **Memory usage climbs on long sessions** | `cacheSize` too large, or many DEM tiles stay in memory | Reduce `cacheSize` (e.g., `cacheSize: 128`). |
| **Contours disappear after a style reload** | The `DemSource` was not re‑registered after `map.setStyle()` | After `map.setStyle()`, call `demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl)` again, then re‑add the contour source/layer. |
| **CORS errors when fetching DEM tiles** | Tiles hosted without `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin:*` | Use a CORS‑enabled host (AWS S3, MapTiler, or your own server with proper headers) or proxy the tiles. |

---

## 10. When to Prefer Pre‑Generated Vector Contours

Runtime contours are great for **dynamic styling** (changing interval, color, or labeling on the fly) and for **offline demos** where you don’t want to ship a separate vector tileset. However, they have limits:

| Use‑case | Runtime contours (maplibre‑contour) | Pre‑generated vector contours |
|----------|-----------------------------------|------------------------------|
| **Very high zoom (≥ 15)** | Requires DEM tiles at that zoom; may become CPU‑heavy. | Already simplified, can be served as low‑zoom vector tiles. |
| **Complex styling (multiple line‑styles, dash patterns, label collisions)** | Possible but may need extra symbol layers; performance still OK. | Vector tiles can embed pre‑computed label placement, reducing client work. |
| **Cross‑platform (mobile, low‑end)** | Worker helps, but still extra CPU. | Pure vector rendering is lighter. |
| **Static map export / PDF** | You must generate the contours first (or rasterize). | Vector tiles are ready to be rendered to PDF. |
| **Data licensing** | Uses the same DEM source you already have; no extra data. | Requires a separate contour dataset (often derived from the same DEM). |

If you need **sub‑meter precision** or **extremely smooth lines** at high zoom, consider generating a vector contour tileset (e.g., with `gdal_contour` or `tippecanoe`) and serving it as a normal `vector` source. For most web‑map applications, the runtime approach is more than sufficient.

---

## 11. TL;DR – Quick Checklist

1. **Choose a DEM source** (`raster-dem`) and note its encoding (`terrarium` or `mapbox`).
2. **Install `maplibre-contour`** (npm or CDN).
3. **Create a `DemSource`** with `url`, `encoding`, `maxzoom`, `worker:true`.
4. **Call `demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl)`** to register the protocol.
5. **Add a contour source** via `demSource.contourSource({ thresholds, elevationKey, levelKey })`.
6. **Add a `line` layer** (and optionally a `symbol` layer) that reads from that source.
7. **Tune `thresholds`**, `minzoom`/`maxzoom`, and `cacheSize` for performance.
8. **(Optional) Enable 3D terrain** with `map.setTerrain()` – the same DEM source works for both.
9. **Test on target devices**; if you see lag, lower `maxzoom` or increase `cacheSize`/disable the worker for debugging.

That’s it – you now have **contour lines generated on the fly** from any raster‑DEM source, fully stylable with MapLibre GL JS, and you didn’t have to pre‑compute a separate vector contour tileset. Happy mapping!
